#text(size: 9.4pt)[Each model below is written to be memorised as a shape, not as a paragraph. The shape is always the same: *a definition in one sentence, the parts named and numbered, and an application to Nigeria.* Three sentences of shape will beat a page of general knowledge.]

#v(6pt)

== G.1 Define the state and name its elements

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* A state is a community of people occupying a defined territory, under a government, and independent of external control. Its elements are: (1) population; (2) territory; (3) sovereignty — supreme authority internally and independence externally; and (4) government, the machinery through which the authority is exercised.

  *The mark-earning move.* Say what the state is *not*: a nation is a people, a society is a wider web of relationships, and a constitution is part of the machinery. One sentence of contrast is worth more than a fifth element.
]

== G.2 Distinguish a written from a rigid constitution

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* A written constitution is one contained in a single codified document, dated and citable; an unwritten one is found in statutes, conventions and cases. A rigid constitution is one that can be amended only by a special procedure, such as a two-thirds majority and the consent of the states; a flexible one can be amended by ordinary legislation.

  *The mark-earning move.* Say that the two tests are independent: Britain's constitution is unwritten and flexible, Nigeria's is written and rigid, and a written constitution could be flexible if it said so.
]

== G.3 Explain the rule of law

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* The rule of law is the doctrine that the government and the governed are subject to the same law, applied by the ordinary courts. It has three limbs: (1) no punishment except for a breach of the law established in the ordinary courts; (2) equality before the law, officials included; (3) the constitution as the result of the ordinary law, so that rights are secured by court decisions rather than by proclamation.

  *The mark-earning move.* Distinguish it from separation of powers, which divides the work, and from constitutionalism, which is the habit of obedience to the rules.
]

== G.4 State the functions of the legislature

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* (1) Law-making, through bills read three times and passed; (2) representation of constituencies; (3) appropriation of public funds; (4) oversight of the executive through committees, questions and investigations; (5) confirmation of appointments; (6) impeachment and removal for misconduct; (7) amendment of the Constitution.

  *The mark-earning move.* Attach the Nigerian number to each: 109 senators, 360 representatives, a two-thirds override, and the Public Accounts Committee as the audit committee of the whole House.
]

== G.5 Distinguish a parliamentary from a presidential executive

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* In a parliamentary system the executive is drawn from the legislature and survives only while it holds a majority there; the Prime Minister is head of government and the monarch or ceremonial president is head of state. In a presidential system the executive and the legislature are separately elected, the President is both head of state and head of government, ministers are appointed from outside the legislature, and the President can be removed only by impeachment.

  *The mark-earning move.* Name the consequence: a vote of no confidence ends a parliamentary government, while a presidential executive can be checked but not dismissed by a simple majority.
]

== G.6 Explain judicial review and judicial independence

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* Judicial review is the power of a court to test an Act or an executive act against the Constitution and declare it void to the extent of any inconsistency. Judicial independence is the arrangement that makes that power usable: security of tenure, salaries charged to the Consolidated Revenue Fund, appointment on the advice of the Judicial Service Commission and the National Judicial Council, and immunity for judicial acts.

  *The mark-earning move.* Distinguish review from appeal: review asks whether the organ had the power, an appeal asks whether the decision was right.
]

== G.7 Distinguish a political party from a pressure group

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* A political party is an organised group that seeks to win power and form a government by contesting elections; it aggregates interests into a programme and recruits the people who will govern. A pressure group is an organised group that seeks to influence policy without seeking office; it is narrower in scope, answerable to its members rather than the electorate, and uses lobbying, petitions, strikes, demonstrations and litigation.

  *The mark-earning move.* The single test: does the organisation nominate candidates? If it does, it is a party.
]

== G.8 Compare the one-, two- and multi-party systems

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* A one-party system permits one legal party: stable but without choice or opposition. A two-party system has two parties that alternate: clear majorities and a clear opposition, but a polarised electorate and neglected minorities. A multi-party system has several parties that can win: broad representation, but coalitions, instability and weaker accountability.

  *The mark-earning move.* Add the Nigerian example: five parties in 1979, a practical two-party alternation in 2015, and eighteen on the 2023 ballot.
]

== G.9 Explain federalism in Nigeria

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* Federalism is a system in which governmental power is divided by a constitution between a central government and the governments of the constituent units, neither level deriving its authority from the other. In Nigeria it is expressed in three legislative lists — exclusive to the federation, concurrent to both (with the federal law prevailing), and residual to the states — and in institutions: 36 states and the FCT, 774 local government areas, the Federation Account and the federal character principle.

  *The mark-earning move.* Give the reasons for adopting it: size, ethnic and cultural diversity, fear of domination, and the wish to keep government close to the people.
]

== G.10 Name the routes to Nigerian citizenship

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* (1) By birth: born in Nigeria to a parent or grandparent of an indigenous community. (2) By descent: born abroad to a Nigerian parent. (3) By registration: a woman married to a citizen, or a person of Nigerian descent on application. (4) By naturalisation: fifteen years' residence, good character, a language of the community and local acceptance. (5) By honorary award, conferred by the President for exceptional service.

  *The mark-earning move.* Say that dual citizenship is permitted, and that registration turns on a connection while naturalisation turns on residence.
]

== G.11 State the determinants of Nigeria's foreign policy

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* (1) National interest — security, prosperity and influence; (2) the economy, above all oil; (3) history, including colonial ties and the civil war; (4) geography, including the neighbours, the Gulf of Guinea and the Sahel; (5) domestic politics and public opinion; (6) the international system; (7) leadership and ideology.

  *The mark-earning move.* Connect the determinants to the doctrine: they explain why Africa is the centre-piece and why the concentric circles put the neighbourhood first.
]

== G.12 Describe the principal organs of the United Nations

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  *Answer.* (1) The General Assembly: all 193 members, one vote each, debating and recommending. (2) The Security Council: fifteen members, five permanent with a veto, responsible for peace and security. (3) ECOSOC: fifty-four members, coordinating the specialised agencies. (4) The Trusteeship Council: suspended in 1994. (5) The International Court of Justice: fifteen judges at The Hague. (6) The Secretariat: the Secretary-General and the staff.

  *The mark-earning move.* Attach one power to each: the Assembly recommends, the Council decides, ECOSOC coordinates, the Court adjudicates and the Secretariat administers.
]

#v(4pt)

#shortcut-box("The shape of every structured answer")[
  + *One sentence of definition.* Never begin with an example; begin with the genus and the difference.
  + *A numbered list of parts.* Three to seven items, each beginning with a verb or a noun, never a paragraph.
  + *One application to Nigeria.* A section, a number or a named institution — the mark that separates a definition from an answer.
  + *One limitation or contrast.* What the thing is not, or where it fails. It is the sentence most candidates leave out and the one examiners reward.
]

#v(8pt)

== G.13 Where the Marks Sit in a Ten-Mark Answer

#text(size: 9.2pt)[The four parts of the shape are not equal in value. An examiner marking a ten-mark question awards, roughly, two for the definition, four for the numbered parts, three for the Nigerian application and one for the contrast. A candidate who writes a beautiful definition and stops has two marks, whatever the length.]

